+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must deliver committed stall-status changes to as many as 800 simultaneous staff browsers while preserving one authoritative event revision for each edit. Connected browsers should see changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity and recover the latest board after disconnections lasting up to 20 minutes.
+
+The delivery mechanism determines more than the browser transport. It shapes the change store's read and publication model, the server's connection and recovery responsibilities, and the browser's synchronization behavior. The choice also establishes an ongoing operating commitment: the project can accept connection-management work, but does not want to couple the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate — No realtime-vendor dependency.** Core board updates must not depend on a hosted realtime provider's API, credentials, service availability, or recurring fees. This eliminates the hosted realtime option.
+- **Update freshness (major).** Prefer prompt delivery of committed changes, with ample headroom beneath the 15-second goal during opening-time bursts.
+- **Operational fit (major).** Prefer an approach whose connection, recovery, and publication work the project is willing to own and operate during published event hours.
+- **Delivery efficiency (moderate).** Avoid unnecessary repeated reads and idle request traffic at 800 simultaneous browsers.
+- **Implementation fit (moderate).** Preserve the stable HTTPS command boundary and make revision-based browser recovery a first-class part of the design.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers request changes over HTTPS every five seconds. The server remains request/response and does not retain socket sessions. The change store exposes cursor-based queries so each browser can retrieve revisions after its last known position, and the browser obtains a current snapshot before advancing its cursor.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **No realtime-vendor dependency: passes.** Delivery uses the same self-hosted HTTPS infrastructure as commands.
+- **Update freshness: partial.** Polling adds an average discovery delay of roughly half the polling interval, plus request and processing time, but can still satisfy the 15-second goal.
+- **Operational fit: partial.** Stateless requests simplify connection operations, but browser recovery and request-capacity behavior still require design and monitoring.
+- **Delivery efficiency: weak.** Every active browser polls even when its board is unchanged, producing repeated reads and idle traffic.
+- **Implementation fit: strong.** Commands and updates remain conventional HTTPS operations, and cursor queries support catch-up without socket-session state.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server accepts WebSocket connections and publishes committed event revisions to subscribed browsers. The change store retains replayable revisions and reliably publishes committed changes. Delivery owns socket capacity, sessions, and reconnect handling; browsers reconnect and catch up from retained revisions before resuming live updates. Commands remain HTTPS requests.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **No realtime-vendor dependency: passes.** The project controls the transport and does not integrate a realtime provider.
+- **Update freshness: strong.** Established connections allow the server to push committed changes immediately rather than waiting for the next browser request.
+- **Operational fit: strong.** The approach directly requires the connection-capacity, reconnect, publication, and recovery operations the project is prepared to own.
+- **Delivery efficiency: strong.** Updates are sent when changes occur instead of every browser repeatedly querying an unchanged board.
+- **Implementation fit: partial.** HTTPS commands and authoritative revisions remain intact, but reliable publication, replay, socket lifecycle management, and browser catch-up add substantial design and implementation work.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The server publishes committed changes to a managed realtime provider, and browsers use that provider's subscription model. Delivery wraps provider integration and reconciles local committed state with provider publication. The provider operates connections and supplies its own recovery and subscription semantics.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **No realtime-vendor dependency: eliminated.** Core updates depend on provider credentials, APIs, service availability, and recurring fees.
+- **Update freshness: strong.** Managed subscriptions provide low-latency delivery without a polling interval.
+- **Operational fit: partial.** The provider removes much of the connection-capacity and reconnect burden, while adding provider monitoring, credentials, reconciliation, and failure handling.
+- **Delivery efficiency: strong.** Event-driven subscriptions avoid idle polling reads against the board's server.
+- **Implementation fit: partial.** HTTPS commands remain unchanged, but the revision and recovery model must be reconciled with provider behavior.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery. This is the only remaining option that combines immediate server-push updates with the project's accepted operating model. Five-second polling is simpler to operate and can meet the freshness goal, but its repeated reads and delivery delay are less attractive than the connection work the project has chosen to accept. The hosted service offers low latency with less connection operations work, but fails the vendor-independence gate.
+
+The decision forecloses polling-only and hosted-provider delivery as the primary update path. It obligates the change store to support replayable revisions and reliable publication, delivery to manage connection capacity and socket sessions, and browser sync to implement reconnect and revision catch-up. Connectivity failures remain visible rather than appearing as an unchanged board, and all edits continue to use the existing authorized HTTPS command boundary.
